  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest surface analysis depicts a weak surface\ntrough transiting central and eastern NC this hour with\nsprawling high pressure centered over the southern plains\nextending in behind it. The trough will do little more than keep\nup winds just enough to hamper more robust radiational cooling\ntonight as well as keep any patchy fog risk at bay. Behind it\nduring daytime hours today, steady NW winds at around 10 mph\nwith higher gusts are likely.\n\nAs we've been mentioning for the past couple todays, tonight\nlooks like the best candidate for fairly widespread frost,\nespecially west of NC 17 as skies remain clear and winds inland\nwill likely be calm with the high cresting overhead. As is\ntypical in these environments deterministic guidance for lows\nare usually not aggressive enough, so blended in NBM 10th\npercentile to knock forecast lows into the mid 30s. Tuesday\nnight is trending slightly warmer with an uptick in cloud cover,\nso probability of frost that night is shifting lower albeit not\nimpossible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance is coming into better agreement for\nincreasingly unsettled weather across the eastern CONUS as\ncomplex mid-level low deepens over the region and steers\nmultiple shortwaves over the mid-Atlantic. Although specific\ndetails remain murky, it is increasingly likely that coastal low\ndevelopment will occur off the eastern coast as early as\nThursday although its intensity and track remain uncertain.\nProbabilistic guidance favors a more northern track, which \nwould keep heavier precipitation away from our area. NBM and \ndeterministic ensembles show this well with overall \nprobabilities of seeing more than a couple tenths of an inch \nunder 20%. \n\nForecast winds have been trending upwards with this system, and\nprobabilities for a period of W to NW winds as high as 25-30 kt\nwith higher gusts have increased from the previous forecast. If\nthis is realized, at least some coastal flooding concerns could\narise along the Outer Banks. Given spread of guidance, it is too\nearly to state the potential magnitude of impacts. Stay tuned\nfor future updates.\n\nBehind the system, unseasonably cool air will flood into the\narea knocking highs down into the 50s for the weekend and offer\nthe first real opportunity for a freeze as 850mb temps fall\nbelow 0 C.",
